                      [«· ToySince by Joo/Toytools ·»]

              Uploads-since-lastcall counter for DayDream BBS

                          Version 1.0  [01-Apr-97]


  Introduction
  ¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯
  ToySince is a door to output uploads (bytes/files) since lastcall to a
  file which can be displayed in ToyJoin or wherever. Can be for example
  used as ToyJoin infolines or displayed when the ToyJoin background is
  displayed.


  Requirements
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~
  Requires a running (Amiga) DayDream BBS and dreamdoor.library v5.


  File Installation
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  Put ToySince to your your Doors directory.


  Usage
  ~~~~~
  Note that there's no need to put this door to DayDream.CFG!

  USAGE: ToySince <CONFIGFILE> <OUTPUTFILE>

  If you specify no parameters, ToySince will just update it's datafile.

  Note! Node number will be added after the outputfile.
  (e.g. if outputfile is "ToySince", the real outputfile is "ToySince.1")


  Configuration
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  The configfile is a normal textfile which can have following codes in it:

     %D    user's last login.
     %nF   files uploaded to conf [n] since lastcall.      (e.g. "%5F")
     %nB   bytes uploaded to conf [n] since lastcall.      (e.g. "%13B")
     %nK   kilobytes uploaded to conf [n] since lastcall.  (e.g. "%2K")
     %nM   megabytes uploaded to conf [n] since lastcall.  (e.g. "%16M")


  Board Installation
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  A new outputfile has to be created after the user logs in.
  Good place for making this is the Welcome.xxx file.

ÿDDoors:ToySince %N|DayDream:Configs/ToySince.CFG DayDream:Display/ISO/ToySince|

  You now have "ToySince.n" (n=node) file that you can display/use somewhere.

  Then you have to update the ToySince datafile when the user logs out.
  Good place for making this is the Goodbye.xxx file.

ÿDDoors:ToySince %N||


  Testing
  ~~~~~~~
  Here's how to test:
  When a user (not you) uploads something and logs out, relogin (without
  logging off) or create the outputfile (see above) some other way.
  Now the outputfile should show how many files/bytes the user uploaded.


  Note
  ~~~~
  ToySince uses byte 47 of the uploadlog for own use (to check if there's
  new uploads).


